This is the most complicated new logic. We're trying to figure out nullability information for a generic parameter based on a type that derives from the class that declared that parameter. When deriving from a generic type, the compiler annotates the derived type with nullable metadata about the parameters used, for example:

```C#
  [Nullable(new byte[] {(byte) 0, (byte) 1, (byte) 1})]
  internal class NonNullableValuesDictionary : Dictionary<string, object>
  {
  }

  [Nullable(new byte[] {(byte) 0, (byte) 1, (byte) 2})]
  internal class NullableValuesDictionary : Dictionary<string, object>
  {
  }
```

Sometimes we need to track through multiple parameters, e. g. in a case like this:
```C#
class TaggedList<TTag, TElement> : List<TElement> { }

class TaggedListOfStrings<TTag> : TaggedList<TTag, string> { }
```

